Simulation Source Code

This directory contains the MATLAB implementations used to model and evaluate cellular connectivity under user mobility.

The simulation framework is organized progressively, from a baseline system-level cellular model to Doppler-aware fading, Monte Carlo evaluation, explicit MIMO modeling, and 5G NR CDL channel modeling.

Directory Structure

src/
├── baseline/
│   └── mobility_cell_eval.m
│
├── doppler/
│   └── mobility_cell_eval_doppler.m
│
└── mimo/
    ├── mobility_cell_eval_mc_MIMO.m
    ├── mobility_SISO.m
    └── mobility_cdL_1x2_MIMO.m

⸻

1. Baseline Cellular Model

baseline/mobility_cell_eval.m

This script implements the baseline system-level cellular mobility simulation.

Main Components

* Multi-cell cellular deployment
* Macro and small-cell configurations
* User mobility at different speeds
* Distance-dependent path loss
* Log-normal shadowing
* Rayleigh small-scale fading
* SINR calculation
* Shannon-based throughput estimation
* Cell association and handover
* Radio Link Failure (RLF) detection
* Simplified MIMO link-budget gain

Experimental Variables

The baseline model evaluates several mobility and deployment conditions, including:

* UE speeds of 30, 90, and 150 mph
* Macro-only deployment
* Macro + small-cell deployment
* Dense small-cell deployment

This implementation serves as the reference model for subsequent channel and antenna extensions.

⸻

2. Doppler-Aware Channel Model

doppler/mobility_cell_eval_doppler.m

This script extends the baseline model by introducing mobility-dependent Doppler effects and time-correlated channel variations.

For a user moving at velocity (v), the maximum Doppler frequency is approximated by

[
f_D = \frac{v}{\lambda}
= \frac{v f_c}{c},
]

where:

* (v) is the UE velocity,
* (f_c) is the carrier frequency,
* (c) is the speed of light,
* (\lambda) is the carrier wavelength.

The Doppler frequency is then used to control the temporal correlation of the simulated fading process.

Channel Model

The simulation combines:

[
\text{Path Loss}
+
\text{Shadowing}
+
\text{Time-Correlated Small-Scale Fading}.
]

The fading process is generated using Doppler-dependent filtering to approximate the temporal correlation introduced by high mobility.

Additional Evaluation

The Doppler-aware implementation evaluates:

* Instantaneous SINR
* Throughput
* Handover events
* Radio Link Failure
* Mobility-induced channel fluctuations
* Monte Carlo performance statistics

This model is used to investigate how increasing UE velocity affects wireless link stability and connectivity.

⸻

3. Monte Carlo MIMO Model

mimo/mobility_cell_eval_mc_MIMO.m

This script extends the Doppler-aware simulation with an explicit antenna configuration and Monte Carlo evaluation.

The default configuration uses:

Transmit antennas: 2
Receive antennas: 2
MIMO configuration: 2 × 2

Unlike the simplified MIMO gain used in the baseline implementation, this model explicitly represents the transmit and receive antenna dimensions in the simulation.

Evaluated Metrics

The Monte Carlo simulations characterize the distribution of:

* SINR
* Throughput
* Handover frequency
* Radio Link Failure probability

under different mobility conditions.

Repeated channel realizations are used to reduce the influence of individual fading realizations and obtain more robust performance estimates.

⸻

4. SISO Baseline

mimo/mobility_SISO.m

This script provides a SISO reference implementation for comparison with MIMO configurations.

The SISO model uses a single transmit and receive antenna and evaluates cellular connectivity under mobility using the same general system-level metrics:

* SINR
* Throughput
* Handover
* Radio Link Failure

The SISO implementation serves as the baseline for evaluating the benefits of additional spatial degrees of freedom.

⸻

5. 5G NR CDL Channel Model

mimo/mobility_cdL_1x2_MIMO.m

This script provides a more advanced channel-modeling implementation based on the 5G NR Clustered Delay Line (CDL) channel model.

The implementation uses MATLAB 5G Toolbox functionality to model a:

1 × 2 MIMO

configuration with a CDL-D channel profile.

Channel Features

The model incorporates:

* 5G NR CDL channel modeling
* Multipath propagation
* Delay spread
* Time-varying fading
* Doppler effects
* Multiple receive antennas
* High-mobility channel variation

The resulting channel model provides a higher-fidelity representation of time-varying wireless propagation than the simplified Rayleigh fading models used in the earlier simulations.

⸻

6. Simulation Progression

The implementations can be viewed as a progressive modeling pipeline:

Baseline Cellular Model
        │
        ▼
Path Loss + Shadowing + Rayleigh Fading
        │
        ▼
Doppler-Aware Time-Correlated Fading
        │
        ▼
Monte Carlo Evaluation
        │
        ▼
Explicit SISO / MIMO Modeling
        │
        ▼
5G NR CDL Channel Model

This progression allows the effects of increasingly realistic channel and antenna models to be studied while retaining a consistent set of system-level performance metrics.

⸻

7. Common Performance Metrics

The simulation scripts primarily evaluate four metrics.

SINR

The signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio is calculated as

[
\mathrm{SINR}

\frac{P_{\mathrm{signal}}}
{P_{\mathrm{interference}}+P_{\mathrm{noise}}}.
]

SINR characterizes instantaneous link quality and is used as the basis for throughput and RLF evaluation.

Throughput

A Shannon-capacity approximation is used to estimate achievable data rate:

[
R = B\log_2(1+\mathrm{SINR}),
]

where (B) denotes the system bandwidth.

Handover

Cell association is updated according to the received-link-quality criterion. A change in the serving cell is recorded as a handover event.

Radio Link Failure

RLF is detected when the simulated link quality remains below a specified SINR threshold for longer than the configured timeout period.

⸻

8. Running the Simulations

Run the scripts directly from MATLAB after adding the src/ directory to the MATLAB path.

For example:

cd src/baseline
mobility_cell_eval

For the Doppler-aware model:

cd src/doppler
mobility_cell_eval_doppler

For the MIMO simulations:

cd src/mimo
mobility_cell_eval_mc_MIMO

The 5G NR CDL implementation requires the MATLAB 5G Toolbox.

⸻

9. Reproducibility

Simulation parameters such as carrier frequency, bandwidth, UE velocity, deployment geometry, shadowing parameters, fading parameters, and RLF thresholds are defined within the corresponding MATLAB scripts.

For reproducible comparisons, the same simulation configuration should be maintained when comparing SISO, MIMO, and different mobility conditions.

⸻

10. Implementation Notes

The models in this directory represent different levels of channel-modeling fidelity.

The baseline and Doppler-aware implementations use system-level approximations suitable for studying the relationship between mobility and cellular connectivity. The CDL implementation provides a higher-fidelity 5G NR channel model.

In particular, the baseline MIMO implementation should be interpreted as a simplified link-budget gain model, while the explicit MIMO implementations model transmit and receive antenna configurations directly.

This distinction is maintained to make the assumptions and modeling fidelity of each simulation transparent.
